course: dictionary
lesson: 6291
title: Moonlight: a light and a job
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Moonlight can be a noun, the light from the Moon at night, or a verb, to do a second job secretly or in your spare time. Read the words around it to decide.
explain: «Then the driver hears another engine behind them, and in the moonlight he sees it crowded with warders waving halberds, policemen waving truncheons and detectives waving revolvers, all shouting "Stop, stop, stop!"» Here moonlight is a noun. It is the light that lets the driver see the crowd. Ask yourself: does the sentence make sense with "the light from the Moon" in place of the word?
explain: «Sunlight takes about 8 minutes 20 seconds to reach the Earth and moonlight about 1.3 seconds.» Science says moonlight is really sunlight bounced off the Moon, because the Moon does not make its own light. Stories and poems use moonlight to make a scene calm, magical or spooky, as in a moonlight walk.
fact: Moonlight (noun) is the light that comes from the Moon at night.
fact: Moonlight is really sunlight reflected off the Moon, because the Moon does not make its own light.
fact: To moonlight (verb, informal) means to do a second job secretly or in your spare time.
fact: Phrases to know: "by moonlight" means in the light of the Moon, and "a moonlight walk" is a walk taken at night under the Moon.
exercise: What does moonlight mean in the first meaning? | A second job you do in your spare time | *The light that comes from the Moon at night | Sunlight that is very hot | A song sung at bedtime
exercise: «Sunlight takes about 8 minutes 20 seconds to reach the Earth and moonlight about 1.3 seconds.» According to the sentence, how long does moonlight take to reach the Earth? | About 8 minutes 20 seconds | *About 1.3 seconds | About one hour | About one whole day
exercise: What does the phrase "by moonlight" mean? | *In the light of the Moon | With no light at all | Very quickly, like a flash | Under the sea
test: The word moonlight can also be a verb. What does it mean when used as a verb? | To sing to the Moon | To shine very brightly at noon | To walk slowly in the dark | *To do a second job secretly or in your spare time
test: «...in the moonlight he sees it crowded with warders waving halberds...» What does the moonlight let the driver do? | Turn the car around | Wave a revolver himself | Hear the engine stop | *See the crowd behind them
exam: Science says moonlight is sunlight bounced off the Moon, so which statement is true? | Moonlight comes from the stars | *The Moon does not make its own light | The Moon makes its own light, just like the Sun | Moonlight is hotter than sunlight
